From 38a7881c9c24a1ff5b517b624e134e10cbd53065 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 18:05:36 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 47/54] nas-relay: Remote Power-ON with a FTDI serial cable

A QMX/QMX+ that is off has no USB device, so PS1; can never reach it. An
FTDI TTL-232R-5V cable on a second NAS USB port drives a bestep PC817 board
across the QMX's VOL encoder switch; holding RTS for 1.8 s is the long press
that turns the radio on.

* Port 3 (TCP 7356) stays connected while the radio is off: PS; -> PS0;,
  PS1; -> PS1; plus a press, no second press for 20 s; port 3 reopens when
  the QMX comes back. Without the cable nothing changes.
* power-on.request in the relay folder presses once (for the Tab5); new or
  touched files count once where DSM will not let the relay delete them.
* No glitch: RTS is set with the FT232R's SIO_SET_MODEM_CTRL request through
  usbfs, ftdi_sio is detached, the cable is never opened as /dev/ttyUSB0;
  RTS released at start-up, after each press and on SIGTERM.
* --power-button auto|off, --power-hold, --power-line, --power-press;
  --probe marks the cable.
* README: "Remote Power-ON with a FTDI Serial Cable" before the Tab5 wired
  Ethernet section; relay README section 4.

Tested with the relay's fake radio and a unit test of the USB requests;
nettest passes. Not yet run on the NAS with the cable and board.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
